module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.emitted_parse_refuses_a_grammatical_input_the_interpreter_accepts

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { CurrentRungUnstatedAfterClimb, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data emitted_parse_refuses_a_grammatical_input_the_interpreter_accepts: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "emitted_parse_refuses_a_grammatical_input_the_interpreter_accepts" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: CurrentRungUnstatedAfterClimb },

  receipts: [
    "**THE EMITTED REALIZATION REFUSES, AT PARSE, A SOURCE THE GRAMMAR ACCEPTS AND THE INTERPRETER PARSES** (INVALID STATE: one source text is fed to TWO realizations of the SAME canonical .dag parse function -- parse_module_prepared_seeded -- and they give DIFFERENT verdicts. Under the interpreter (the native test route, native_test_context_from_ingest -> native_census_module_resolution) the text PARSES and refuses only later at LOWERING with else_less_if_unlowered. Under the emitted gen-1 binary (the native CLI acquisition route, acquisition_parsed -> parse_module_prepared -> parse_module_prepared_seeded) the SAME text refuses at PARSE with parse_g0_tokens_remain. The text is a module whose single fn body is an ELSE-LESS if followed by a tail statement: `fn f(c: Bool, i: Int) -> Int {` / `  if c { i }` / `  i` / `}` (the rtp_guard_fallthrough_source fixture, byte-identical across the three cc2-ceprobe runs, member file sha256 0f785394e803ce694370e7c917d7faad069b70bfabc2eb2167cd2160dcf409a8; emit.err sha256 837b2a71). The interpreter is RIGHT: the grammar production dag_grammar_if_block_form is `seq(fn_body, optional(if_else_tail))` (dag.dag:2414), so an else-less `if c { i }` is a valid block-form member and the trailing `i` is the next statement; the whole module parses and the else-less refusal belongs at lowering)",

    "**LOCALIZATION (underway; every link below is decided by the emitted fragment or the built-run differential, none asserted)** -- algebra first: the module production is a sequence(), which accepts only when EVERY element accepts, and parse_expr_repeat (parse.dag:4286) ALWAYS returns Accepted. So the only way a well-formed module reaches `accepted but remaining non-empty -> parse_g0_tokens_remain` is an emitted link that lets the module ACCEPT while leaving the tail statement unconsumed, and the emitted table.refusal stays Absent on this path (so it is NOT a refusal). RULED OUT BY READING, against the seed-emit fragments (v2_compiler_parse.rs sha 188439f2, v2_compiler_tokenize.rs sha b6e4e2a0, v2_std_compilers_lexing.rs sha 9503c390, srv2 cc2-seedemit): the rc-guard path (analyze_rc_pattern 05_emit_rust.dag:11276 leaves needs_rc_pattern=false; emit_typed_match_arm 15698 emits a correct no-guard discriminant match); the refusal setters (the emitted file has the SAME three setters as the .dag, none on this path); parse_expr_repeat_step and parse_current_position (emitted faithful: continue iff head.start+1 differs, else stop); the Map carrier (map_insert=rc_map_insert / map_lookup=v1_rt::lookup over the persistent im::HashMap keyed by i64 -- structural-sharing, so a correctly-recorded key is always found; no Rc get_mut aliasing-drop); and the layout-UNKNOWN branch (it would SET a refusal -> ProductionRunRefused, not tokens_remain). LEADING LINK UNDER TEST: parse_expr_after_line_break (parse.dag:4425, reached only via the single PreparedAfterLineBreak wrapper at parse.dag:4612) gates an Accepted element on token_stream_line_break_before; a NoLineBreakInGap yields a PLAIN Rejected (empty diags, no refusal) that the repeat treats as done, leaving the tail -> exactly this symptom. THE OPEN TENSION (why this row stays RungUnread): the static read shows the whole lexing/layout/line-break chain faithful AND the self-host parses thousands of multi-statement bodies, so no mechanism explains a fault that is at once faithful-emitted and specific to else-less-if; therefore the localization is NOT closed by reading and is decided by the built-run differential (ce_probe_twostat_text / ce_probe_sameline_text: a general second-statement drop vs an if/optional-specific one vs a line-break-dependent one)",

    "**WHAT MAKES IT RECURRING** (one shared emitted realization -- not one grammar rule): the leading link, parse_expr_after_line_break / token_stream_line_break_before, is reached through the single PreparedAfterLineBreak wrapper for EVERY statement in EVERY module, so a wrong decision there is one class across the whole corpus, not one rule; and the grammar's other combinators (the ~50 dag_grammar_optional sites, the choice sites such as dag_grammar_if_expr's seq(expr, choice(if_then_form, if_block_form)) at dag.dag:2441, the repeat and terminal matchers) all reach ONE emitted realization built by the seed emitter (src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag) from the .dag grammar interpreter. A WHOLESALE break is self-excluded: the emitted compiler parses thousands of well-formed modules, so the fault is specific to the shape where that shared realization's decision flips the verdict -- which is why a construct that is rare in the corpus (a statement following an ELSE-LESS if, where else-less-if is usually block-final) can stay latent in the self-host yet be a real defect",

    "**WHY IT IS NOT AN ABSENCE OF COVERAGE** (the interpreter is RIGHT, so no interpreted claim reddens -- the same blind spot the freemonoid list_init miscompile had, whose fix comment records 'THE INTERPRETER LOWERS ALL OF THESE CORRECTLY, which is why no interpreted claim discriminates the class'). A text-match emitted witness (compile_dag_rust_emit_check) CANNOT distinguish the two verdicts, because BOTH the wrong and the right realization compile and emit; per the nested_pattern row's N7-1 rule a text check 'cannot tell the two apart, so a probe written with it would be a decoration.' The only witness that discriminates is a BUILT-RUN differential: the same source bytes run through the interpreter AND the freshly-emitted gen-1 binary, asserting one parse verdict -- and THIS change adds exactly that witness: three interpreter-side probe fns on v2.test.claim.self_host.closure_emission (ce_probe_elseless_text, ce_probe_twostat_text, ce_probe_sameline_text) feed the verbatim source through the interpreter's own parse authority, paired with the emitted gen-1 emit of the same bytes; the twostat and sameline controls separate a general second-statement drop from an if/optional-specific one and from a line-break-dependent one. Per DESIGN section 353 these stay enrolled as permanent regression controls once the wall lands",

    "**RUNG FOUND AT** rung 1 -- one observed divergence between the two realizations on one real grammar input, reproduced three times on srv2 (cc2-ceprobe / ceprobe2 / ceprobe3, at main 0dc96e1e, seed gunbc 9e5ee601, gen-1 CLI 3ffe505e), with the interpreted control green (namespace_xl0 return_tail_position) and the emitted side red. No interpreter claim discriminates it, and no per-input built-run differential exists in the durable set yet",

    "**TRIGGER** the both-executor discriminating probe: build the seed gunbc, emit the gen-1, build the emitted crate, and run BOTH executors on the verbatim else-less member, expecting the SAME verdict (parse-OK -> refuse at lowering with else_less_if_unlowered). Red while the emitted realization is wrong; green once it matches the interpreter. Durable interpreted side: ce_probe_elseless_text in v2.test.claim.self_host.closure_emission.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE** wherever a construct is realized independently by the interpreter and by the emission target, ask what fact decides its behaviour and where that fact is DECLARED. For the grammar's combinators the deciding facts are the parse-result discriminants and the token classes the interpreter reads; any emitted arm or plan that decides on anything OTHER than those -- a first-token commit where the interpreter falls back, a field compare, an arm-order tie-break, an absent case that propagates instead of accepting -- is a candidate for this class. Do NOT special-case one grammar rule (do not name if_else_tail): the class is the shared emitted realization of a grammar combinator diverging from the interpreted .dag on one input.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.namespace_xl0.return_tail_position", decl_name: "an_else_less_if_that_falls_through_still_refuses_as_unlowered", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.namespace_xl0.return_tail_position", decl_name: "an_else_less_if_as_the_last_statement_still_refuses_as_unlowered", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
